Z||000011||Ken Wittlief||05-14-2001||08:57 AM||||that would have been the final admission of guilt for Pontiac, if they had installed a Halon Fire Extinquisher system in the engine compartment with a button on the dash to set it off (captian, we've got a fire in engine #1!)<P>  I would guess the horn, since its a doorbell button (temp contact).<P> I often had problems with old VWs - the backup switch on the tranny would fail - no backup lights, required for instection, so I would put a toggle switch on the dashboard - which was legal here in NY.<P>  It provided a great way to freak out the person behind you at a red light. Put the car in 1st gear and switch on the backup lights. When the light is about to change start reving the engine up like your gonna dump the clutch.<P> If you've never seen the 'oh-my-god-he's-got-it-in-reverse-and-doesnt-know-it' look on someones face in your rearview mirror, then you have been missing out.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||05-14-2001||08:59 AM||||I friend of mine had a camero IROC, and we were discussing how worthless seatbelts are at 160mph one day - decided what you really need at that speed is a sunroof and ejection seats.<P>  Dan thought about it for a while and then said, "I would only go along with that if the car could be equipped with an 'in-tunnel or under-bridge' detector.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||1
